S('Background', 'What is a Neural ODE?', True, ['A neural network f gives the rate of change of a hidden state: dz/dt = f(z, t, theta)', 'The state at any time = starting state + integral of f', 'A numerical ODE solver computes the integral', 'Continuous time: the state can be read at any time, even irregular ones'], '', 'A residual network updates its state in discrete layers. Make the steps infinitely small and you get an ODE whose rate of change is a neural network. A numerical solver, such as Runge-Kutta, integrates it step by step.', 'Equation to show: dz(t)/dt = f(z(t), t, theta).')
S('Background', 'ODE solvers and the adjoint method', False, ['The solver takes small steps; more steps = more accuracy and more computation', 'The paper trains with the adjoint method (a backward ODE) to save memory', 'In our project: fixed-step RK4, gradients through the solver steps, no adjoint'], '', 'The paper proposes the adjoint method to get gradients with constant memory. We use a fixed-step solver and backpropagate directly through it, which is fine for our small models.')
S('Background', 'From Neural ODE to Latent Neural ODE', True, ['Observations -> encoder -> initial latent state z0', 'z0 -> Neural ODE -> latent state at every time', 'latent state -> decoder -> predicted (x, y)', 'Variational: z0 is a distribution (VAE-style), trained with the ELBO'], '', 'A Latent ODE wraps the Neural ODE in an encoder and a decoder. The encoder reads sparse observations and produces a starting state; the ODE evolves it; the decoder draws the path.')
