S('Results', 'Result E: controlled 2x2 ablation', True, ['A 8/64, B 16/64, C 8/128, D 16/128; identical data streams; one run each', f"Reconstruction: B {pct(rel('B', 'overall', 'recon_rmse'), 0)}, C {pct(rel('C', 'overall', 'recon_rmse'), 0)}, D {pct(rel('D', 'overall', 'recon_rmse'), 0)} vs A", f"Forecast: B {b_pct}, D {d_pct}; C {c_pct} (not significant)", 'Interaction positive overall: the two changes reinforce each other'], FG + 'ABLATION/fig5_ablation_overall.png', 'Bigger encoders reconstruct better. For forecasting only the latent-size changes look worse; widening the GRU alone made no clear difference. I stress each bar is a single run.')
S('Results', 'Result F: forecast error over the horizon', True, ['Larger models are better just after t = 7 and worse later', f"Crossing above A at t = {V['cross']['B']:.2f} (B), {V['cross']['C']:.2f} (C), {V['cross']['D']:.2f} (D)", f"Window 9-10: A {V['win'][('A', (9, 10))]:.3f}, D {V['win'][('D', (9, 10))]:.3f}", 'Single seed; cause not established'], FG + 'FORECASTING/fig6_forecast_error_vs_time.png', 'This is why I am careful with the words "worse forecasting". Right after the forecast starts the larger models are better; they overtake model A around t = 8 and end worse.')
S('Results', 'Why we are careful: effect size vs run-to-run variation', True, [f"Same architecture, two runs: {rA} and {rD} difference in forecast error", f"Five seeds (two-family): forecast {min(ssf['values']):.3f} to {max(ssf['values']):.3f}, CV about {ssf['std'] / ssf['mean'] * 100:.0f}%", f"Ablation forecast effects: {b_pct}, {c_pct}, {d_pct}", 'Hence: exploratory; multi-seed runs are the next step'], FG + 'ABLATION/fig7_effects_vs_seed_variation.png', 'Retraining the same recipe changes forecasts by about twenty percent, which is as large as the ablation effects. So these are hints, not findings yet.', 'The groups come from different experiments; shown on one axis for scale only.')
S('Results', 'Result G: dynamics conditioning', False, ['Extra 4-D code c controls the ODE (M3) vs shared M1 and parameter-matched control M2', f"Circle forecast: M1 {cond('seed_level.M1.circle.forecast_rmse.mean', '.3f')}, M2 {cond('seed_level.M2.circle.forecast_rmse.mean', '.3f')}, M3 {cond('seed_level.M3.circle.forecast_rmse.mean', '.3f')}; specialists {cond('gap_to_specialists.circle.specialist_forecast_rmse', '.3f')}", '5 seeds: M3 not better than M1 or M2', 'Hypothesis not supported at this scale'], FG + 'FORECASTING/conditioning_fig5_model_comparison.png', 'The code was used, but it did not close the gap to specialists.')
S('Results', 'Result H: synthetic-to-real transfer (GeoLife)', True, [f"Median error (30 obs): linear {gl('interpolation.30.linear.median', '.1f')} m, PCHIP {gl('interpolation.30.pchip.median', '.1f')} m", f"Frozen synthetic model {gl('interpolation.30.frozen_three_shapes.median', '.1f')} m; GeoLife-trained {gl('interpolation.30.geolife_scratch.median', '.1f')} m; fine-tuned {gl('interpolation.30.geolife_finetuned_from_three_shapes.median', '.1f')} m", f"Fine-tuned beats scratch only at 30 observations (ratio {V['pa'][30]['gmr']:.2f}); worse at 10, 20, 40, 50", 'Single run per model; data and weights not redistributed'], FG + 'GENERALIZATION/fig8_geolife_transfer.png', 'Straight lines beat all of our learned models on real GPS. Synthetic training did not transfer, and real-data training helped but not enough.')
S('Discussion', 'Key findings', True, ['1. Shared models forecast worse than specialists (5 vs 3 runs)', '2. No zero-shot generalisation to dissimilar families', '3. Larger encoders reconstruct much better (single seed)', '4. Forecast vs size: mixed, late-horizon, exploratory', '5. No transfer to real GPS'], '', 'These five findings come with different strengths of evidence; the first has multiple runs, the others are single-run.')
S('Discussion', 'Limitations', True, ['Mostly single seed; variation about 20%', 'Synthetic data, one noise level and observation protocol', 'Only naive baselines; no sequence-model baseline', 'Future is not supervised; the objective hypothesis is untested', 'Not an exact replication; no improved forecasting method'], '', 'These limitations define what we can and cannot claim.')
S('Discussion', 'Future work', True, ['1. Multi-seed repeat of the 2x2 ablation (A and D first)', '2. Stronger baselines (per-family curve fit, sequence model)', '3. Leave-one-family-out generalisation', '4. Test supervising the future (a labelled diagnostic)', '5. Only then: evaluate a candidate improvement'], '', 'The first step is multi-seed confirmation; everything else depends on it.')
S('Discussion', 'Conclusion', True, ['Adapted re-implementation and extension of Latent ODEs', 'One shared model works but costs forecast accuracy; no zero-shot generalisation; no transfer to real GPS', 'Encoder size: better reconstruction; forecasting exploratory', 'No improved forecasting model has been developed'], '', 'To conclude: we have a verified framework and clear negative results; the main positive result is reconstruction from larger encoders. Whether encoder size changes forecasting still needs multi-seed evidence.')
S('Discussion', 'Thank you / Questions', True, ['Thank you', 'Questions'], '', 'Thank you. I am happy to take questions.')

W('09_PRESENTATION_CONTENT/COMPLETE_SLIDE_OUTLINE.md', '''
# Complete slide outline

%d slides (core slides marked **core**; the rest are backup/appendix and can be skipped for a shorter talk). Figure paths are relative to the package root. Suggested time: about 1 minute per slide for core slides.

%s

**Design notes for the slide builder.** Use plain white or light backgrounds; put exact numbers in tables (CSV files are in `06_EXPERIMENTAL_RESULTS/RESULTS_TABLES/`); put "single seed" on every result slide that is single-seed; never use the words "replication", "improved model" or "proves".
''' % (len(SL), MD(['#', 'Section', 'Slide title', 'Core?', 'Figure'], [[s['n'], s['section'], s['title'], 'core' if s['core'] else 'backup', ('`' + s['fig'] + '`') if s['fig'] else '-'] for s in SL])))

sb = ['# Slide-by-slide content', '', 'For each slide: title, text to put on the slide, figure, and notes. Exact numbers are final; all are traceable in `11_SOURCE_INDEX/`.', '']
for s in SL:
    sb += [f"## Slide {s['n']}: {s['title']}  ({'core' if s['core'] else 'backup'})", '', f"*Section: {s['section']}*", '']
    sb += [f'- {b}' for b in s['bullets']]
    sb += ['', f"**Figure:** {('`' + s['fig'] + '`') if s['fig'] else 'none'}"]
    if s['note']: sb += ['', f"**Note:** {s['note']}"]
    sb += ['']
W('09_PRESENTATION_CONTENT/SLIDE_BY_SLIDE_CONTENT.md', '\n'.join(sb))
sp = ['# Speaking script', '', 'Spoken text for every slide (first person). Roughly 30 to 45 seconds per core slide. Say "single run" whenever a result comes from one training run.', '']
for s in SL: sp += [f"## Slide {s['n']}: {s['title']}", '', s['script'], '']
W('09_PRESENTATION_CONTENT/SPEAKING_SCRIPT.md', '\n'.join(sp))

QA = [
 ('Is this a replication of the Neural ODE paper?', 'No. It is an adapted re-implementation and extension. We re-implemented the Latent ODE architecture but used our own synthetic data, a fixed-step solver and different objective details; we did not reproduce the paper\'s numbers.'),
 ('Did you develop a better forecasting model?', 'No. No method that improves forecasting was developed or tested. We studied how an existing model behaves across families and sizes.'),
 ('What is new compared with the 2018 paper?', 'The paper\'s latent-ODE experiment uses two spiral directions. We evaluate five families, shared vs specialist models, zero-shot tests, model-size studies and real GPS. Whether this is novel relative to post-2018 work was not checked (needs a literature review).'),
 ('Why a Latent ODE rather than an ordinary RNN?', 'It models continuous time, so irregular observation times are natural and the state can be queried at any time. We did not train an RNN baseline, so we cannot say it forecasts better than an RNN.'),
 ('Why do you encode backwards in time?', 'It follows the paper: reading newest to oldest means the final hidden state has just read the earliest observation, which anchors the initial latent state at t = 0.'),
 ('Why is the ODE time-invariant?', 'It follows the paper, so that the latent state at any time determines the future. Any time dependence must be expressed through the state.'),
 ('Which solver, and is it accurate?', 'torchdiffeq rk4 (Runge-Kutta 3/8 rule, fourth order), step 0.1. Between solver steps values are linearly interpolated; we measured the effect on RMSE at about 2e-4, negligible against differences of 0.03 to 0.5.'),
 ('Why no adjoint method?', 'The models are small, so direct backpropagation through the solver is cheap enough; the adjoint is a memory optimisation.'),
 ('What exactly does the training loss supervise?', 'Only the 30 observed noisy points (Gaussian likelihood, sigma 0.05) plus KL. The future is never supervised. A variant that supervises the future exists in the code but was never run.'),
 ('How were checkpoints selected? Was the test set used?', 'By lowest validation negative ELBO. Validation forecast RMSE is logged but not used; the test set is never used for selection. Selection picks epochs 294-299 of 300, so models were not fully converged.'),
 ('Why 30 observations, always including t = 0?', 'A design choice for a fixed observation budget; t = 0 anchors the initial state. Models trained with 30 observations work only near 30 (validation reconstruction 0.061 at 30 vs 0.494 at 20).'),
 ('What do reconstruction and forecast RMSE measure?', 'Reconstruction: error at the 110 unobserved times within t <= 7. Forecast: error at the 60 times with t > 7. Per-trajectory RMSE in original units, averaged over trajectories.'),
 ('Are your simple baselines strong enough?', 'They are deliberately simple. Stronger baselines (a per-family curve fit, a sequence model) were not run; this is listed as future work.'),
 ('Does the shared model beat specialist models?', f"No. Forecast error is {pct(SVR('circle', 'forecast_rmse')['rel'], 0)} (circle) and {pct(SVR('spiral', 'forecast_rmse')['rel'], 0)} (spiral) higher for the shared model (5 vs 3 runs); every shared run is worse. Only the circle difference is significant after correcting for multiple tests."),
 ('Does the model generalise to unseen families?', 'No for figure-eight and sinusoid (worse than hold-last), partial for ellipses (direction and rough forecast, shape wrong). There is no leave-one-family-out experiment.'),
 ('Why is the five-family model worse than the three-family model on circle, spiral, ellipse?', 'Observed: higher error (circle 0.1401 -> 0.2594 forecast). But training set size and optimiser steps also changed (33,000 vs 19,800), so it cannot be attributed to the number of families alone. Single run.'),
 ('What does the 2x2 ablation show?', f"Bigger encoders reconstruct much better. Over the full forecast window the latent-size changes are worse (B {b_pct}, D {d_pct}); widening the GRU alone is not clearly different (C {c_pct}, p = {pv(cP['wilcoxon_p_holm'])}). Single seed; the differences are the size of run-to-run variation."),
 ('Does increasing capacity worsen forecasting?', 'Not established. In one set of runs it is associated with worse late-horizon forecasts; the larger models are better early in the forecast window. We cannot claim causation or generality without multi-seed runs.'),
 ('What is the interaction between latent dimension and GRU width?', f"Raising the latent size changes forecast error by {AB['paired']['B_minus_A/overall/forecast_rmse']['mean_difference']:+.3f} with GRU 64 but {AB['paired']['D_minus_C/overall/forecast_rmse']['mean_difference']:+.3f} with GRU 128, so they reinforce each other overall (positive interaction {fI['mean']:+.3f}); for the sinusoid the sign is opposite."),
 ('Is it overfitting?', 'Classical overfitting (validation loss rising while training loss falls) was not observed. The training loss, however, only supervises observed points, so extrapolation is not directly trained; that explanation is a hypothesis that has not been tested.'),
 ('Why do larger models forecast worse later in the horizon?', 'Not known. Possible explanations (untested): faster error growth in larger latent spaces, the observed-only objective, optimisation differences.'),
 ('How reliable are the p-values?', 'They quantify variation across test trajectories for the specific trained models. They do not capture variation across training seeds, which was only measured in the two-family studies (forecast CV about 23%).'),
 ('Why did conditioning not help?', 'In 5 seeds the conditioned model was not better than the shared model or the parameter-matched control. The code was used (it controls radial growth) but did not improve forecasts. The conditioned model was not tuned separately.'),
 ('Why did you test on GeoLife?', 'To check whether anything learned on synthetic geometry transfers to real movement. It does not: straight-line interpolation (7.1 m median) is far better than the frozen model (126.4 m) and the GeoLife-trained models (47 to 61 m).'),
 ('Does synthetic pre-training help on real data?', f"Only at 30 observations (fine-tuned / scratch ratio {V['pa'][30]['gmr']:.2f}); at 10, 20, 40 and 50 observations it is worse. Single run per model."),
 ('Where are the classification and CNF reproductions?', 'They are not in this repository. They must come from the earlier presentation and be labelled as separate work.'),
 ('What would you do next?', 'Multi-seed repeat of the 2x2 ablation (A and D first, about 80 min per run), stronger baselines, leave-one-family-out tests, and a test of supervising the future before proposing any improvement.'),
 ('How reproducible is your work?', '106 unit tests pass; training scripts never overwrite checkpoints; every result is saved with its configuration; an independent audit recomputed the ablation statistics from raw predictions and found no discrepancies.'),
 ('What hardware did you use?', 'CPU only: AMD EPYC 7313 (32 threads), 125 GB RAM; about 80 minutes per 300-epoch run with 4 threads.'),
 ('Why synthetic families rather than real data first?', 'Synthetic data give known ground truth for the future, controlled difficulty and exact family labels for generalisation tests.')]
W('09_PRESENTATION_CONTENT/POSSIBLE_SUPERVISOR_QUESTIONS.md', '# Possible supervisor questions and answers\n\nAnswers are consistent with the verified results; where something was not done or not verified, the answer says so.\n\n' + '\n\n'.join(f'**Q{i + 1}. {q}**\n\n{a}' for i, (q, a) in enumerate(QA)))
